  "response": "The adjudication proceeded by testing two fully specified alternatives against each independent body of evidence. Marija Gimbutas formulated the Kurgan hypothesis, placing the Proto-Indo-European homeland in the Pontic\u2013Caspian steppe. Colin Renfrew's Anatolian hypothesis, by contrast, tied Indo-European dispersal to the spread of farming out of Anatolia around 7000 BCE. Renfrew's Anatolian hypothesis located the homeland in the Balkans and dated the dispersal to 5000 BCE; a dispersal carried out of Anatolia by farmers around 7000 BCE and a Balkan origin at 5000 BCE cannot both be true of the same proposal, so one of the two characterizations must be set aside before the model can be tested at all.\n\nLexical reconstruction supplied the chronological lever that made the dispute decidable. Reconstructed Proto-Indo-European contains an inherited term for wheel, *k\u02b7ek\u02b7los, and because each branch inherits rather than borrows this lexeme, the material record of wheeled transport bears directly as evidence on the age of the parent speech community. Since the earliest wheeled vehicles are archaeologically attested no earlier than about 3500 BCE, that terminus indicates that Proto-Indo-European remained a unified language after the mid-fourth millennium BCE. Radiocarbon dates place the earliest wagon burials in the sixth millennium BCE, a result that cannot be squared with a terminus of about 3500 BCE for the first wheeled vehicles; the two chronologies cannot both stand, and comparativists had to determine which calibration to discard.\n\nA further line of argument turned on the status of the oldest documented branch. Even though the Anatolian branch lacks much of the wheeled-vehicle terminology reconstructed for the other branches, Hittite is the earliest attested Indo-European language, recorded in cuneiform in the second millennium BCE. Specialists weighed that asymmetry carefully, since a branch may be the first to be written down without being the last to leave the parent community, and the lexical gap was read as a signal of early separation.\n\nQuantitative phylogenetics was recruited as a further arbiter, and its estimates moved as its assumptions were tightened. Gray and Atkinson's 2003 Bayesian phylogenetic analysis yielded a root age of roughly 8700 years before present. Chang and colleagues subsequently imposed ancestry constraints on the language phylogeny in 2015 and obtained a root age near 6000 years before present. The sequence of these published estimates became part of the methodological record on which later reviewers drew.\n\nArchaeogenetics then entered the argument with a different kind of datum. Haak and colleagues reported in 2015 that Corded Ware individuals derived roughly three-quarters of their ancestry from Yamnaya-related populations; whereas in the eastern Mediterranean the picture is otherwise, for ancient DNA from Bronze Age Anatolia shows no detectable Yamnaya steppe ancestry. Either the Bronze Age Anatolian genomes contain no detectable Yamnaya steppe component, or those same genomes carry substantial Yamnaya steppe ancestry: exactly one of these must hold of the sequenced material, and it cannot be both. Resolving which obtains was the pivot on which the genomic contribution to the homeland question ultimately rested.",
